(** A single R-base-graphics-style plot, spelled [dm::Plot] in user code.

    Covers R's high-level plotting calls ([plot()], [hist()], [barplot()], [boxplot()], [pie()],
    [curve()], [qqnorm()], ...) as constructors and static factories, plus the "add to the
    current plot" verbs R exposes as separate functions ([points()], [lines()], [abline()],
    [legend()], [text()], ...) as chainable member functions. A plot is an ordinary value: it
    owns copies of its data, copies cheaply enough to pass around, and draws nothing until it is
    rendered with to_svg(), save(), or show().

    Two composition styles mix freely. Setters such as set_title() and verbs such as points()
    mutate the plot and return [RPlot&] for chaining; the pipe [plot | dm::points(...)] (see
    compose.hpp) appends a reusable layer to a copy. A fresh plot has the title "datamunge
    plot", axis labels "x" and "y", a 960x640 canvas, and a light grid.

    {b Example:}

    {@cpp[
    const auto x = dm::seql(-2.0, 2.0, 9);
    auto plt = dm::Plot(x, x * x)
                   .set_title("Parabola")
                   .set_xlab("x")
                   .set_ylab("x squared");
    plt.lines(x, x * x, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}).abline_h(0.0);
    plt.save("parabola.svg");
    ]}

    C++ class [datamunge::graphics::RPlot].

    Derives from {!Plot}: it passes wherever those are expected, and [(x :> plot)] or
    [Plot.upcast x] upcasts it explicitly. Inherited functions are repeated here.

    @see "dm::points()" C++ [dm::points()]
    @see "dm::legend()" C++ [dm::legend()]
    @see "dm::Histogram" C++ [dm::Histogram]
    @see "dm::Splom" C++ [dm::Splom]
    @see "RLayout" C++ [RLayout] *)
