(** Minimises an expensive black-box function on a box with few evaluations.

    The loop is: 1. evaluate [initial_samples] points drawn uniformly in [[lower, upper]]; 2.
    repeat [max_iterations] times: fit the surrogate to all observations, score [candidates]
    uniform random points with BayesianSurrogate::acquisition() (the incumbent being the
    smallest value so far), polish the best one by compass search on the acquisition (steps of
    10% of each side, halved [refine_iterations] times, clipped to the box), and evaluate the
    objective there.

    Only point evaluations of the objective are used, so it suits expensive simulations,
    experiments and hyperparameter tuning where each evaluation costs far more than the
    surrogate fit.

    {b Example:}

    {@cpp[
    struct Bowl : dm::optim::ArbitraryFunction {
        double evaluate(const std::vector<double>& x) override { return (x[0] - 1.0) * (x[0] - 1.0); }
    } bowl;
    dm::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate gp({.fit_hyperparameters = true});
    const auto result = dm::optim::BayesianOptimization({.initial_samples = 4, .max_iterations = 10})
                            .run(bowl, {-3.0}, {3.0}, gp);
    std::print("{:.3f} after {} evaluations\n", result.best_point[0], result.values.size());  // 1.002 after 14 evaluations
    ]}

    {b References:}

    {ul
    {- D. R. Jones, M. Schonlau and W. J. Welch (1998). "Efficient global optimization of expensive black-box functions". Journal of Global Optimization 13, 455–492.}
    {- B. Shahriari et al. (2016). "Taking the human out of the loop: a review of Bayesian optimization". Proceedings of the IEEE 104(1), 148–175.}}

