(** Truncated hierarchical B-spline (THB-spline) of Giannelli, Juttler and Speleers.

    Level 0 is the caller's TensorBSpline; level l + 1 is the dyadic refinement of level l. A
    dyadic cell tree defines the nested domains Omega^0 (whole domain) contains Omega^1 contains
    ..., where Omega^l is the union of cells of depth >= l. A level-l basis function is active
    when its support lies in Omega^l but not in Omega^(l+1). The THB basis consists of the
    active functions, each truncated: its representation on finer levels drops the finer
    functions whose support lies in the finer refined domain. The resulting basis is a partition
    of unity, preserves the continuity of the underlying splines, and spans the hierarchical
    spline space.

    Internally each level stores a full coefficient net whose entries outside the next level's
    refined domain always equal the refinement of the coarser level; coefficient edits are
    pushed down through all finer levels with truncation, so evaluation needs only the finest
    level covering a point. Refinement never changes the represented function. Bases must be
    clamped. Cells are integer ids (root 0).

    {b Example:}

    {@cpp[
    auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(2, 4);
    dm::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline thb(dm::fda::TensorBSpline({b, b}, std::vector<double>(16, 1.0)));
    thb.refine_region({0.0, 0.0}, {0.5, 0.5}, 1);
    std::print("{} {:.3f}\n", thb.level_count(), thb.evaluate({0.2, 0.2})); // 2 1.000
    ]}

    {b References:}

    {ul
    {- C. Giannelli, B. Juttler and H. Speleers (2012). THB-splines: The truncated basis for hierarchical splines. Computer Aided Geometric Design 29(7), 485-498. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cagd.2012.03.025}
    {- D. R. Forsey and R. H. Bartels (1988). Hierarchical B-spline refinement. https://doi.org/10.1145/378456.378512}}

    C++ class [datamunge::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline].

    @see "HierarchicalBSpline" C++ [HierarchicalBSpline]
    @see "dyadic_refine()" C++ [dyadic_refine()] *)
