    values use exp(-2{i n}D^2). Mean and sd must be fixed independently of this sample: fitting
    them here invalidates the stated calibration. This is not a Lilliefors test. Ties are
    accepted, but continuous-null calibration is affected by rounding. O(n log n) work/O(n)
    storage, input unchanged, no omission/shared state.

    {b Example:}

    {@cpp[
    const auto k=dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal({-1,0,1});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",k.parameter1); // 3
    ]}

    {b References:}

    {ul
    {- https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html}}

    C++:
    [HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal(const std::vector<double> & x, double mean = 0.0, double sd = 1.0, Alternative alternative = TwoSided)]

    @param x At least two finite observations.
    @param mean Finite prespecified normal mean, default zero. (optional; C++ default [0.0])
    @param sd Positive finite prespecified normal SD, default one. (optional; C++ default [1.0])
    @param alternative TwoSided (default); Greater tests empirical CDF above reference, Less
      below (opposite to a location alternative). (optional; C++ default [TwoSided])
    @return statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n and approximate p_value; no CI.
    @raise Invalid_argument Too few/nonfinite values, invalid mean/sd or alternative. (C++
      [std::invalid_argument])
    @see "HypothesisTestResult" C++ [HypothesisTestResult] *)
val ks_test_one_sample_normal : ?mean:float -> ?sd:float -> ?alternative:Alternative.t -> float array -> hypothesis_test_result

