    @param x Nonempty finite first sample.
    @param y Nonempty finite independent second sample.
    @param alternative TwoSided (default); Greater tests F_x above F_y, Less below, so Greater
      corresponds to stochastically smaller x. (optional; C++ default [TwoSided])
    @return statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n_x, parameter2=n_y and approximate p_value; no CI.
    @raise Invalid_argument Empty/nonfinite samples or invalid alternative. (C++
      [std::invalid_argument])
    @see "HypothesisTestResult" C++ [HypothesisTestResult] *)
val ks_test_two_sample : ?alternative:Alternative.t -> float array -> float array -> hypothesis_test_result

(** Shapiro-Francia composite normality test, like R nortest::sf.test().

    Squared correlation of sorted observations with Blom normal scores, using Royston (1993)
    log- normal p-value approximation. Tests some normal distribution; a large p-value does not
    prove normality. Rounding/ties affect calibration. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage; input
    unchanged, no omission/shared state.

    {b Example:}

    {@cpp[
    const auto s=dm::stats::shapiro_francia_test({1,2,3,4,5});
    std::print("{}\n",s.has_conf_int); // false
    ]}

    {b References:}

    {ul
    {- https://search.r-project.org/CRAN/refmans/nortest/html/sf.test.html}}

    C++:
    [HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::shapiro_francia_test(const std::vector<double> & x)]

    @param x Between 5 and 5000 finite, nonconstant observations.
    @return statistic=estimate1=W-prime and approximate p_value; no CI.
    @raise Invalid_argument Invalid sample size, nonfinite input or constant sample. (C++
      [std::invalid_argument])
    @see "HypothesisTestResult" C++ [HypothesisTestResult] *)
val shapiro_francia_test : float array -> hypothesis_test_result

(** Adjust a vector of p-values for multiple comparisons, matching R's p.adjust().

    {ul
    {- Bonferroni: p\[i\] * n, capped at 1 -- simplest and most conservative FWER control.}
    {- Holm: step-down Bonferroni (Holm-Bonferroni) -- uniformly more powerful than plain Bonferroni while still controlling FWER, no extra assumptions.}
    {- Hochberg: step-up FWER control -- more powerful than Holm, but assumes the test statistics are independent (or positively dependent).}
    {- Hommel: step-up FWER control, uniformly more powerful than Hochberg under the same independence assumption; the most expensive to compute (O(n^2)). For n == 2 this degenerates to (and is computed as) Hochberg, matching R.}
    {- BH (Benjamini-Hochberg, aka "fdr" in R): controls the false discovery rate under independence or positive regression dependence -- the standard choice for large- scale testing (e.g. genomics, many simultaneous hypothesis tests).}
    {- BY (Benjamini-Yekutieli): controls the FDR under arbitrary dependence; more conservative than BH.}
    {- None: returns the input p-values unchanged.}}

    As in R, a single p-value (n <= 1) is always returned unchanged regardless of [method].

    C++:
    [std::vector<double> datamunge::stats::p_adjust(const std::vector<double> & p, PAdjustMethod method = BH)]

    @param p the C++ argument [p]
    @param method_ the C++ argument [method] (optional; C++ default [BH]) *)
val p_adjust : ?method_:PAdjustMethod.t -> float array -> float array

(** Westfall-Young single-step minP adjustment (Westfall & Young, 1993) -- a resampling- based
    FWER correction that accounts for the actual dependence structure between tests (unlike the
    analytic methods above, which either assume independence or make no assumption at all).

    [resampled_p] is a B x m matrix: row b holds the m p-values recomputed on the b-th
    permutation or bootstrap resample (generated by the caller -- this function is purely the
    combinatorial engine, not the resampling scheme itself). [sorted_p] is the m original
    p-values sorted ascending, and column k of every row in [resampled_p] must correspond to the
    same hypothesis as sorted_p\[k\] (i.e. the caller sorts once and reorders both consistently
    before calling).

    Single-step: every hypothesis is compared against the same reference distribution -- the
    per-resample minimum p-value taken over {i all} m hypotheses -- so adjusted p\[k\] =
    (fraction of resamples whose overall minimum p-value <= sorted_p\[k\]). This is
    automatically monotonic in sorted_p and valid under subset pivotality, but less powerful
    than the step-down refinement below since it never narrows the comparison set as hypotheses
    are resolved.

    Returned in the same (ascending sorted_p) order as the input; the caller is responsible for
    mapping back to original hypothesis order if needed.

    C++:
    [std::vector<double> datamunge::stats::westfall_young_adjust(const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & resampled_p, const std::vector<double> & sorted_p)]

    @param resampled_p the C++ argument [resampled_p]
    @param sorted_p the C++ argument [sorted_p] *)
val westfall_young_adjust : float array list -> float array -> float array

(** Romano-Wolf step-down resampling adjustment (Romano & Wolf, 2005) -- like
    westfall_young_adjust, but more powerful: at each step k (in ascending sorted_p order), the
    reference distribution is the per-resample minimum p-value taken only over the {i remaining}
    hypotheses k..m-1, narrowing by one hypothesis at each step rather than always using all m.
    This is the step-down generalization that controls FWER without requiring subset pivotality.
    Monotonicity (adjusted p\[k\] >= adjusted p\[k-1\]) is enforced explicitly, since narrowing
    the comparison set at each step means it isn't automatic the way it is for the single-step
    procedure above.

    Same input/output conventions as westfall_young_adjust: [resampled_p] is a B x m matrix with
    column k aligned to sorted_p\[k\], and the result is returned in the same sorted order.

    C++:
    [std::vector<double> datamunge::stats::romano_wolf_adjust(const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & resampled_p, const std::vector<double> & sorted_p)]

    @param resampled_p the C++ argument [resampled_p]
    @param sorted_p the C++ argument [sorted_p] *)
val romano_wolf_adjust : float array list -> float array -> float array

(** One-proportion score test with an uncorrected Wilson confidence interval.

    Tests a fixed Bernoulli success probability using the signed null-standard-error z
    statistic; statistic stores z^2 but the chosen tail uses its sign. The optional continuity
    correction affects the test only: its Wilson interval is uncorrected and need not invert
    that test. Approximation requires sufficiently populated success/failure counts. O(1)
    work/storage, no shared state.

    {b Example:}

    {@cpp[
    const auto p=dm::stats::proportion_test_one_sample(45,100);
    std::print("{:.2f}\n",p.estimate1); // 0.45
    ]}

    {b References:}

    {ul
    {- https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/prop.test.html}}

    C++:
    [HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::proportion_test_one_sample(std::size_t successes, std::size_t n, double p = 0.5, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, bool correct = true, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)]

    @param successes Integer successes in \[0,n\].
    @param n Positive independent-trial count.
    @param p Finite null probability strictly in (0,1), default .5. (optional; C++ default
      [0.5])
    @param alternative TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for true probability relative to p.
      (optional; C++ default [TwoSided])
    @param correct True (default) applies the capped half-count correction to z. (optional; C++
      default [true])
    @param conf_level Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95. (optional; C++ default
      [0.94999999999999996])
    @return statistic=z^2, parameter1=1, estimate1=successes/n, p_value and uncorrected Wilson
      confidence bounds.
    @raise Invalid_argument Invalid counts, null probability, alternative or confidence level.
      (C++ [std::invalid_argument])
    @see "HypothesisTestResult" C++ [HypothesisTestResult] *)
val proportion_test_one_sample : ?p:float -> ?alternative:Alternative.t -> ?correct:bool -> ?conf_level:float -> int -> int -> hypothesis_test_result

(** Pooled score test for two proportions, with an unpooled difference interval.

    Tests equality using the pooled null SE and optionally a capped continuity correction. The
    difference CI uses an unpooled Wald SE and an uncapped half-count width correction when
    requested; it is approximate and is not a Wilson/Newcombe interval. All-zero/all-one pooled
    samples return z=0,p=1. O(1) work/storage, no shared state.

    {b Example:}

    {@cpp[
    const auto p=dm::stats::proportion_test_two_sample(2,10,3,10);
    std::print("{:.1f}\n",p.estimate1); // 0.2
    ]}

    {b References:}

    {ul
    {- https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/prop.test.html}}

    C++:
    [HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::proportion_test_two_sample(std::size_t successes1, std::size_t n1, std::size_t successes2, std::size_t n2, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, bool correct = true, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)]

    @param successes1 First success count in \[0,n1\].
    @param n1 Positive first independent-trial count.
    @param successes2 Second success count in \[0,n2\].
    @param n2 Positive independent second-trial count.
    @param alternative TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for p1-p2. (optional; C++ default
      [TwoSided])
    @param correct True (default) corrects the score test and adds a half-count CI width.
      (optional; C++ default [true])
    @param conf_level Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95. (optional; C++ default
      [0.94999999999999996])
    @return statistic=z^2, parameter1=1, estimates=sample proportions, p_value and confidence
      bounds for p1-p2, clamped to \[-1,1\].
    @raise Invalid_argument Invalid counts, alternative or confidence level. (C++
      [std::invalid_argument])
    @see "HypothesisTestResult" C++ [HypothesisTestResult] *)
val proportion_test_two_sample : ?alternative:Alternative.t -> ?correct:bool -> ?conf_level:float -> int -> int -> int -> int -> hypothesis_test_result

(** Exact binomial test with Clopper-Pearson confidence bounds, like R binom.test().

    One-sided p-values are inclusive tails. Two-sided p-values sum PMFs no larger than the
    observed PMF, using relative comparison tolerance 1e-7. The equal-tailed Clopper-Pearson
    interval has at-least-nominal coverage but need not invert the probability-ordered two-sided
    test. Null endpoints zero/one are supported. Two-sided enumeration costs O(n), intervals use
    bounded beta-CDF bisection; O(1) auxiliary storage, no shared state.

    {b Example:}

    {@cpp[
    const auto b=dm::stats::binomial_test(9,10);
    std::print("{:.6f}\n",b.p_value); // 0.021484
    ]}

    {b References:}

    {ul
    {- https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/binom.test.html}}

    C++:
    [HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::binomial_test(std::size_t successes, std::size_t n, double p = 0.5, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)]

    @param successes Integer observed successes in \[0,n\].
    @param n Positive independent Bernoulli trial count.
    @param p Finite null probability in \[0,1\], default .5. (optional; C++ default [0.5])
    @param alternative TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for true success probability.
      (optional; C++ default [TwoSided])
    @param conf_level Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95. (optional; C++ default
      [0.94999999999999996])
    @return statistic=successes, parameter1=n, estimate1=successes/n, p_value and
      Clopper-Pearson interval; one-sided intervals end at zero/one.
    @raise Invalid_argument Invalid counts, probability, alternative or confidence level. (C++
      [std::invalid_argument])
    @see "HypothesisTestResult" C++ [HypothesisTestResult] *)
val binomial_test : ?p:float -> ?alternative:Alternative.t -> ?conf_level:float -> int -> int -> hypothesis_test_result

(** One-sample Student t-test of a population mean, like R t.test().

    Independent normal observations give t=(mean-mu)/SE with n-1 df. The CI estimates the
    population mean, not mean-mu. Inputs are copied only for paired differences; all routines
    leave inputs unchanged and use no shared mutable state. O(n) work; no null/nonfinite
    omission.

    {b Example:}

    {@cpp[
    const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_one_sample({1,2,3,4});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.parameter1); // 3
    ]}

    {b References:}

    {ul
    {- https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html}}

    C++:
    [HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::t_test_one_sample(const std::vector<double> & x, double mu = 0.0, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)]

    @param x At least two finite observations with positive representable sample variance.
    @param mu Finite null mean, default zero, in observation units. (optional; C++ default
      [0.0])
    @param alternative TwoSided (default), Less or Greater, relative to mu. (optional; C++
      default [TwoSided])
    @param conf_level Finite confidence level strictly in (0,1), default .95. (optional; C++
      default [0.94999999999999996])
    @return statistic=t, parameter1=df, estimate1=sample mean, p_value and mean confidence
      bounds; remaining scalars retain defaults. One-sided intervals have one infinite endpoint.
    @raise Invalid_argument Too few/nonfinite values, invalid mu/alternative/confidence or
      zero/nonfinite standard error. (C++ [std::invalid_argument])
    @see "HypothesisTestResult" C++ [HypothesisTestResult] *)
val t_test_one_sample : ?mu:float -> ?alternative:Alternative.t -> ?conf_level:float -> float array -> hypothesis_test_result

