    Welch uses unequal sample variances and Satterthwaite approximate df; normality alone does
    not make its reference exact. Pooled Student requires equal population variances and
    independent normal observations for its exact reference. CI estimates mean(x)-mean(y).
    O(n_x+n_y) work, inputs unchanged, no shared mutable state or omission.

    {b Example:}

    {@cpp[
    const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_two_sample({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.estimate1); // 2
    ]}

    {b References:}

    {ul
    {- https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html}}

    C++:
    [HypothesisTestResult datamunge::stats::t_test_two_sample(const std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y, bool equal_variance = false, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996)]

    @param x At least two finite observations in the first group.
    @param y At least two finite independent observations in the second group.
    @param equal_variance False (default) uses Welch; true uses pooled variance. (optional; C++
      default [false])
    @param alternative TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for mean(x)-mean(y). (optional; C++
      default [TwoSided])
    @param conf_level Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95. (optional; C++ default
      [0.94999999999999996])
    @return statistic=t, parameter1=df, estimate1/2=group means, p_value and confidence bounds
      for their difference. One-sided bounds can be infinite.
    @raise Invalid_argument Invalid sample sizes/values, alternative/confidence, unrepresentable
      mean difference or zero/nonfinite combined standard error. (C++ [std::invalid_argument])
    @see "HypothesisTestResult" C++ [HypothesisTestResult] *)
